    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        393,
        -53.57397611470779
      ],
      "size": [
        759.984375,
        247.203125
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Fisheye calibration. A wide-angle lens does not obey the pinhole model cv2.calibrateCamera assumes, so cv2.fisheye has its own calibrator with FOUR coefficients (k1..k4) and its own undistortion. Three things this workflow makes visible, each measured on these exact views. (1) CALIB_RECOMPUTE_EXTRINSIC is not optional: the top row runs the same eight boards with and without it and gets 0.13 px RMS / fx 320 against 22 px / fx 390 - the second one is garbage and nothing in the output says so except the RMS. (2) The balance knob of 'estimate new K' trades field of view against black corners, and cv2's estimator returns an ALL-NaN matrix when the fit left k3/k4 unconstrained (which it does here, at a perfectly good 0.13 px RMS) - so the middle row calibrates again with CALIB_FIX_K3 | CALIB_FIX_K4 and only that branch can drive balance. The node itself never emits NaN: it falls back to the original K and logs. (3) The bottom row runs the PINHOLE calibrator on the same views. It fits them just as well (0.13 px) - over the middle of the frame, which is all these boards cover, five Brown-Conrady coefficients can imitate a fisheye. The models disagree where there is no data: undistorting the frame corners with each puts them hundreds of thousands of pixels apart, because the pinhole polynomial diverges outside the region it was fitted on. The last row feeds blank frames in: no board, no calibration, found=false instead of a raise."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
